==English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|la|en}} {{term|ex||lang=la|from}} + {{term|post||lang=la|after}} + {{term|facto||lang=la}}, ablative of {{term|factum||lang=la|deed}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj|pos=[[ex]] [[post]] [[facto]]|-}}

# [[retroactive|Retroactive]]
# {{legal}} [[formulate|Formulated]] or [[enacted]] after some [[event]], and then retroactively [[applied]] to it.
#*{{quote-book|title=Retroactive Legislation‎|page=56|author=Daniel E. Troy|year=1998|passage=Coupled with the Constitution's proscription of '''ex post facto''' laws is a similar prohibition against bills of attainder.}}
#*{{quote-news|work=Barron's|date=Saturday, March 21|year=2009|title=Would a Tax on Bonuses Be Constitutional?|author=Jim McTague|passage=That the tax would take effect after some of the payments were made also raises issues. Says former Attorney General Richard Thornburg, also in an e-mail: "Such legislation could well run afoul of constitutional restrictions on bills of attainder, '''ex post facto''' laws, laws impairing the obligations of contract, unauthorized takings of property and the like."}}

